WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.330
A lot of people tend to think the c2 as
the ultimate goal of language learning

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:08.220
but the truth is that speak in a foreign
language at a C2 level and speaking like

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.920
a native speaker are two different
things.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.340
Hi, my name is Luca from LucaLampariello.com and today I'm going to show you

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:20.449
the difference between speaking on a C2
level and speaking like a native speaker.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.949
Have you ever caught yourself using
expressions such as "he speaks like a

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:30.539
native speaker, he sounds like a native
speaker" but the reality is that speaking

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:35.160
and native speakers don't go well
together in one sense: native speaker is

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.140
about IDENTITY, it is not just about the
knowledge, it's not just about the skills

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:44.129
A native speaker is a native speaker and
if you're a foreigner, if you're a

00:00:44.129 --> 00:00:48.329
non-native speaker, well, you're not a
native speaker. That's an important point

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:52.550
to take because a lot of people strive
for speaking like a native speaker,

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.730
sounding like a native speaker, but the
reality is that this is just a mere

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:02.250
emulation. Just to give you an example,
let's take a Sanish professor or a

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.810
French professor who has been living in
the US for a long time and speaks

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:12.659
English really well. So well, in fact, that
he gives lectures at university and he

00:01:12.659 --> 00:01:17.369
is much appreciated by his students. Well, that Danish professor speaks

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:22.650
English has acquired native-like skills
so he can speak at a native-like level.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.759
He can express himself very well but
he's not a native speaker. On the other

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:31.890
hand, let's take a peasant, a person who
is a native speaker still - he was born in

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.299
the United States - but has no formal
education. Who do you think speaks

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:41.939
English better between the two? Obviously the peasant probably speaks English

00:01:41.939 --> 00:01:47.280
worse. He cannot express himself as
eloquently and as elegantly as the

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.710
professor but the reality is that he is
still a native speaker in this show

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:58.560
So who speaks English better? The Danish professor or the American peasant? On the

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.520
one hand, the Danish professor can
express himself very eloquently, can

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.869
speak really well, can write essays. The
peasants, though, although he cannot do

00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:11.700
all these things still has an advantage,
and the advantage is that he was born in

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:18.120
the United States so
he has incorporated all the customs, ways

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.550
of saying, ways of being, facial features
gestures, experiences that the Danish

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:28.290
professor probably didn't have because
he did acquire knowledge, he did acquire

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:34.500
the skill of expressing himself elegantly
and eloquently but he was not born there.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:39.810
He did not have the same experiences so
you can learn to speak almost like a

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:44.280
native but bear in mind that you will
never BE a native speaker. You can

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:49.620
EMULATE the status of a native speaker
without being one. The people who get

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.180
very close to speaking and being 
native speakers are normally the people

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.890
who live 30 to 40 years in a country and
whose kids were born in that

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.880
country, so they've seen the entire thing,
maybe from an external perspective, they

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.480
didn't go to the kindergarten, but
they've seen their kids go to

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.350
kindergarten and interact with other
kids and interact with other adults and

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:14.760
they've seen adults themselves
interacting with kids going to school,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.220
going to high school etc Those get close
to speaking and being native speakers

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.210
but again, they will never be native
speakers because they were not born in

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.110
that country.

00:03:29.129 --> 00:03:34.659
So if there is one thing for which
people can say or can think that you are

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:40.030
a native speaker is accent. If you speak
a given language very very well, to the

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:43.450
point where people cannot tell that
you're a foreigner, well, that means that

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:46.720
you have reached native-like
pronounciation, and if you want to reach

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.590
native-like pronunciation in a given
language, you have to take into account

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.310
other things. People normally tend to
think okay well what I have to do is

00:03:54.310 --> 00:04:00.370
focus on the text, focus on sounds,
replicate sounds, repeat etc. True but I

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:04.480
think that one of the most important
factors that is often neglected is that

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.549
you have to take everything into account,
the entire "communication tool/kit" that every

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:15.519
single native speaker has, and by
"communication tools" I mean my hand,

00:04:15.519 --> 00:04:19.870
co-speech gestures, my face, how I'm
communicating with my face, my eyes my

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:23.289
body, everything, the whole thing. Just to
give you a few examples

00:04:23.289 --> 00:04:26.800
an Italian - might sound like a stereotype
but we actually do that -

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.310
actually might say "ma che stai a fa?" This is in Rome. Right. (it means): "what are you doing?"

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:34.330
We tend to use that especially when we ask questions with an interrogation mark

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:38.500
"Ma che stai a fa?" or a Spanish would say

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.680
"Pero que te pasa macho" (What's going on dude)? the entire expression.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.919
Or "Sag mal, biste bescheurt?" (are you stupid or what). Or the French would go like

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:49.479
Bon je sais pas s'il faut aller au cinéma j'ai pas envie" (I don't know whether to go to the movies, I don't feel like it)

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:54.699
These are just a few samples of how different cultures
channel different ways of saying things

00:04:54.699 --> 00:04:59.949
and the accent does count, your
pitch, your intonation, your articulation

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:05.830
your pronunciation, but the truth is your
hands, your face ,your eyes do count and

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:09.909
if you want to speak and sound pass
off as an native speaker even if you're

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:14.199
not, pay attention to these, watch movies,
pay close attention to how people

00:05:14.199 --> 00:05:18.280
communicate with each other, adult with
adult, kid with kids, pay attention to

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:21.460
every single detail, because they DO
count and you can learn these

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:27.930
consciously and unconsciously.

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:32.469
It should be fairly obvious by now that
speaking like a native or passing off

00:05:32.469 --> 00:05:37.059
like a native is not just a matter of
sounds. It's a holistic process, but there

00:05:37.059 --> 00:05:40.439
is one third factor which makes a
difference and it is cultural

00:05:40.439 --> 00:05:44.680
understanding. How many times have we
found ourselves talking to native

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.460
speakers and you've seen two native
speakers talking about stuff that you

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:54.509
had no idea of? You know, no clue, and this
can refer to, I don't know, people on TV

00:05:54.509 --> 00:06:00.909
or like TV series or maybe TV shows, or
something that happened, historical things

00:06:00.909 --> 00:06:05.710
geographical things etc etc This is
part of culture this is the part of

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:11.319
heritage that a given generation of
native speakers has and - bear in mind - a

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:17.409
given generation of speakers - because
language learning its hues its its

00:06:17.409 --> 00:06:21.159
cultural references - they do change in
space and in time.

00:06:21.159 --> 00:06:26.139
So if we're speaking for example about
English, Brits maybe don't understand

00:06:26.139 --> 00:06:29.919
certain cultural references of Americans
for Americans certain things of the Brits and

00:06:29.919 --> 00:06:34.449
maybe the same Americans of now might
not understand things that were very

00:06:34.449 --> 00:06:39.339
common 200 years ago (in the US). That's reason why
it's so difficult to nail a language,

00:06:39.339 --> 00:06:44.050
because it changes in time, it changes in
space, it changes its cultural references

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:48.069
That's why it's so difficult when we
talk about being a native speaker

00:06:48.069 --> 00:06:51.550
Actually we should talk about being a
native speaker of that place and of that

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.669
time and that's that's the cultural
references that you will never be able

00:06:54.669 --> 00:06:58.899
to replicate because you were born in
another time or maybe in the same time

00:06:58.899 --> 00:07:03.819
but unfortunately in another place. I
just wanted to say before we wrap up the the

00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:06.879
video, that this is not supposed to be
discouraging, I don't want to just put

00:07:06.879 --> 00:07:10.809
you down and say "hey you will never be a
native speaker" it's just a fact. You will

00:07:10.809 --> 00:07:14.769
not be a native speaker but you can pass
off as one. This happened to me many

00:07:14.769 --> 00:07:18.909
times and I can guarantee you that even
if I'm not American, I'm not French and I

00:07:18.909 --> 00:07:22.899
will never be - not German for that matter
or any other nationality, I've had an

00:07:22.899 --> 00:07:26.529
amazing time every time people thought
that I was one of them

00:07:26.529 --> 00:07:31.839
because through sound you can actually
embrace another reality, embrace another

00:07:31.839 --> 00:07:37.899
culture and and.. embrace an historical
moment people communities, you name it! So

00:07:37.899 --> 00:07:40.540
it's always worth speaking in a foreign
language,

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:44.290
it's always worth passing off as a
native, if you can, if that's your aim

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:48.760
apart from speaking, you know, at an
excellent level, because it opens worlds,

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:56.890
it makes your life richer in so many
ways you cannot even start to fathom

00:07:56.890 --> 00:08:01.010
So the first point to take into account
is that speaking like a native does not

00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:04.700
equal being like a native because being
like a native is just a unique

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.300
characteristic of native speakers and
that's fine! The second point to take

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:12.500
away is that if you want to sound like a
native speaker, you should not only speak

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:16.430
like a native speaker that is with good
articulation and pronunciation, but also

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:21.980
acts like one with co speech gestures reactions,
facial features, you name it! It's just

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:25.610
like a little bit like acting and the
third thing if you want to pass off as a

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:29.150
native speaker is that you should
integrate and being able to understand

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:33.590
the same cultural and historical
references and maybe from TV and TV

00:08:33.590 --> 00:08:39.530
series etc that native speakers do, in a
given space and in a given time. If you

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:42.770
liked the video and you haven't
